From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] asap7/cva6: the RAMs as upstream has them, made macros by
 AUTO_MEMORIES

asap7/cva6 rewrote cva6's three RAM wrappers to instantiate fakeram
macros: the D-cache data RAM lost its byte enables, so every refill and
store hit overwrote the other way's word (ORFS #4769), the I-cache RAMs
lost theirs, and the I-cache read behaviour became a fakeram's in place
of tc_sram's, all without a check.

sram_cache.sv, hpdcache_sram.sv and hpdcache_sram_wbyteenable.sv are
upstream's again, unmodified, with upstream's behavioural
hpdcache_sram_1rw and hpdcache_sram_wbyteenable_1rw. AUTO_MEMORIES
makes each RAM a macro, cva6.memories naming its pins and byte enables:
the D-cache data (128 x 64, 8 byte enables) and directory (64 x 28).
The I-cache's RAMs sit behind sram_cache's techno cut, which upstream
fills with pulp's generic tc_sram; SYNTH_VERILOG_SURGERY
(asap7/cva6/surgery.py) puts a single-port SRAM with byte enables there
in its place, the I-cache data (64 x 256, 32 byte enables) and tag
(64 x 25, 4 byte enables, the last of them 1 bit). The fakeram views
leave the config.

asap7/cva6-regfile wants the RAMs too, and its own surgery moved
ariane_regfile's array; one script carries both now, the register file
behind --ariane-regfile, since the flow stages only the script
SYNTH_VERILOG_SURGERY names.

The README says what still differs from upstream: tie-offs for outputs
cv32a65x leaves undriven, outside this change.

Not upstreamed until tested; retires at a //:bump onto an ORFS that
carries it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
